message.content:
Here’s a compact way to wire everything up in a **single** invocation of `manageReactState`.  
(Feel free to format across multiple physical lines – it remains one logical statement.)

```js
// One‑liner init of the React state manager
const myAppStore = manageReactState(
  /* config */{
    initialState : initialStateObject,
    reducers     : reducersMap,
    middlewares  : [loggerMiddleware],
    enhancers    : ["applyMiddleware('myMiddleWare')"]          // enhancer list
  },
  /* store id/name */        "myAppStore",
  /* context provider/consumer */  MyAppContext,
  /* custom hooks */         { selector: useStateSelectorHook, dispatch: useDispatchActionHook }
);
```

*Explanation*

| Argument | What goes here |
|----------|----------------|
| `config.initialState` | Your starting state (`initialStateObject`) |
| `config.reducers` | Map of reducer callbacks (`reducersMap`) |
| `config.middlewares` | Array of middleware functions (`[loggerMiddleware]`) |
| `config.enhancers` | Enhancer expressions – e.g., `"applyMiddleware('myMiddleWare')"`. |
| Second arg (`store`) | Identifier / key used internally (“myAppStore”). |
| Third arg (`context`) | A React Context instance created elsewhere (`MyAppContext`). |
| Fourth arg (`hooks`) | Object exposing your custom hooks (`{ selector, dispatch }`). |

With this setup, your React components can consume the store via `MyAppContext.Provider` and interact through `useStateSelectorHook` & `useDispatchActionHook`.

